<h2>The problem the case addresses</h2>
<p>The DV Act builds a two-document intake. The aggrieved person presents an application under Section 12 setting out the domestic relationship and the acts of domestic violence; the Protection Officer prepares the Domestic Incident Report in the statutory form. The two serve different functions — the application is the party's pleading, the DIR is the machinery's structured record — but because the DIR arrives in a familiar printed format, trial courts sometimes treat it as the measure of the case. That is what happened here: the complaint set out instances of abuse, taunting and dowry-related humiliation attributed to the two family members, but the DIR's columns said little about them, and both the Magistrate and the appellate court took the DIR as decisive.</p>

<h2>What the High Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>The DIR is a supporting document</h3><p>It has no independent standing in a pending complaint. It can neither substitute nor supersede the original complaint, and the two must be read together when the court decides whom to summon.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Absence in the DIR is not absence of a case</h3><p>Where the Section 12 application itself sets out allegations falling within the definition of domestic violence in Section 3, the lack of corresponding particulars in the DIR is no ground to refuse summons.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The DIR here was not even silent</h3><p>The Court noted that the DIR's section on verbal and emotional abuse in fact mentioned all the respondents in connection with insults and ridicule over dowry — underlining how thin the basis for deletion was.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Protective statute, protective reading</h3><p>The lower courts' approach, the Court held, frustrated the DV Act's purpose. A remedial statute for women facing violence in the household cannot be narrowed by the formatting of an intake form.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Summoning is not condemnation</h2>
<p>The judgment is equally instructive for respondents. Because Section 12 proceedings are civil in character, being summoned is not the equivalent of being charged with an offence. The respondents' answer to the allegations comes next: replies, affidavits and, where sought, discharge from the array if the material discloses nothing against them. The decision moves the filter from the summoning stage — where only a prima facie reading of the complaint and DIR together is involved — to the stage where the court actually has both sides' material. For family members of the husband who find themselves named, the working consequence is that the contest over their continuance in the case is fought on the complaint's specific allegations, not on the DIR's brevity.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>For the aggrieved person: plead specifically in the Section 12 application itself — dates, acts and the role of each respondent — and do not rely on the Protection Officer's form to carry the case. If the DIR omits material allegations, point the Magistrate to the complaint and to this judgment. For respondents seeking deletion: the route is to demonstrate that the complaint itself, read as a whole, attributes nothing to the particular respondent that falls within Section 3 — an argument about the pleading, not about the DIR. And for both sides: orders on summoning are appealable under Section 29, but as this case shows, the appellate court must apply the same complaint-first reading.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this article summarises a judgment of the High Court of Delhi for general information. It is not legal advice, and it does not relate to any matter handled by this chamber.</p>
</div>
